WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:02.521
This is the Wattieza tree. 

00:00:02.521 --> 00:00:06.424
Widely believed to be the&nbsp;first tree that ever existed. 

00:00:06.424 --> 00:00:08.847
Fossils show&nbsp;that it appeared on Earth&nbsp;

00:00:08.847 --> 00:00:12.097
around 385 million&nbsp;years ago &nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.097 --> 00:00:15.200
when life on land was just beginning to flourish.

00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:18.208
But if we wind back the clock just&nbsp;a bit more

00:00:18.208 --> 00:00:20.200
 Earth looked very different.

00:00:20.200 --> 00:00:22.934
 There&nbsp;were no lush forests, no soil,

00:00:22.934 --> 00:00:27.134
and grass wouldn't&nbsp;exist for another 300 million years.

00:00:27.134 --> 00:00:31.034
 It was almost&nbsp;impossible 
for anything to survive on land.

00:00:31.034 --> 00:00:34.000
So&nbsp;where did trees come from? 

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:36.500
Despite this desolate&nbsp;landscape, 

00:00:36.500 --> 00:00:40.714
there was one strange form of 
life that&nbsp;towered over everything. 

00:00:40.714 --> 00:00:42.934
These are prototaxites.

00:00:42.934 --> 00:00:47.734
Giant living organisms that once stood up to 25 ft&nbsp;tall. 

00:00:47.734 --> 00:00:50.534
Although they look like early trees, 

00:00:50.534 --> 00:00:54.334
these&nbsp;mysterious creatures weren't trees at all. 

00:00:54.334 --> 00:00:59.080
Instead&nbsp;of being made from an 
organized network of long&nbsp;woody fibers,

00:00:59.080 --> 00:01:03.634
prototaxites had a messy network&nbsp;of interwoven tubes.

00:01:03.634 --> 00:01:05.211
Unlike trees,

00:01:05.211 --> 00:01:07.401
they didn't use&nbsp;photosynthesis to survive 

00:01:07.401 --> 00:01:10.934
and instead got all of&nbsp;their nutrients from the ground. 

00:01:10.934 --> 00:01:12.160
Because of this,&nbsp;

00:01:12.160 --> 00:01:15.934
scientists generally thought 
they were some kind&nbsp;of giant fungus. 

00:01:15.934 --> 00:01:18.655
But a recent study of a prototaxite fossil

00:01:18.655 --> 00:01:21.622
showed that the organic compounds&nbsp;left behind

00:01:21.622 --> 00:01:23.934
had no resemblance to fungi at all.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.934 --> 00:01:30.067
In fact, it seems like prototaxites were&nbsp;
an entirely different form of life&nbsp;altogether.

00:01:30.067 --> 00:01:31.656
For millions of years, 

00:01:31.656 --> 00:01:33.867
these things&nbsp;dominated the landscape 

00:01:33.867 --> 00:01:37.366
and helped pave the way 
for trees to eventually take over.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.366 --> 00:01:39.200
But how? 

00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:41.411
At&nbsp;some point in Earth's history, 

00:01:41.411 --> 00:01:43.900
all life existed&nbsp;in the ocean. 

00:01:43.900 --> 00:01:45.948
Without soil or nutrients,&nbsp;

00:01:45.948 --> 00:01:49.120
land was an impossible place to survive.&nbsp;

00:01:49.120 --> 00:01:51.053
But deep inside the rocks,

00:01:51.053 --> 00:01:54.533
the nutrients needed&nbsp;to support life did exist. 

00:01:54.533 --> 00:01:57.034
It was just a matter of&nbsp;getting them out. &nbsp;

00:01:57.034 --> 00:01:58.748
With the help of waves&nbsp;

00:01:58.748 --> 00:02:01.488
complex&nbsp;life forms like fungi and algae 

00:02:01.488 --> 00:02:03.929
got pushed closer to the water's edge &nbsp;

00:02:03.929 --> 00:02:07.034
and started to&nbsp;settle on the rocky shores. 

00:02:07.034 --> 00:02:08.080
From there

00:02:08.080 --> 00:02:10.359
the fungi used their powerful acids 

00:02:10.359 --> 00:02:14.800
to break down&nbsp;the rock and 
release the nutrients trapped inside.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.800 --> 00:02:17.679
The algae used these nutrients to grow&nbsp;

00:02:17.679 --> 00:02:20.640
and in exchange gave the fungi special sugars&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:22.934
that it made through photosynthesis.&nbsp;

00:02:22.934 --> 00:02:27.049
The two organisms had formed 
a close symbiotic&nbsp;relationship

00:02:27.049 --> 00:02:29.534
and started spreading further&nbsp;inland. 

00:02:29.534 --> 00:02:34.389
Over time, the fungi and algae 
eventually&nbsp;died and decomposed,

00:02:34.389 --> 00:02:38.600
leaving behind a loose, thin layer of organic matter. 

00:02:38.600 --> 00:02:44.280
The&nbsp;Earth's surface now had the key ingredient that&nbsp;would allow life to explode onto the land...

00:02:44.280 --> 00:02:45.760
Soil.

00:02:45.760 --> 00:02:47.959
Not only was it full of nutrients,

00:02:47.959 --> 00:02:49.853
it acted like&nbsp;a sponge, 

00:02:49.853 --> 00:02:52.400
allowing water to be stored inside.&nbsp;

00:02:52.400 --> 00:02:56.667
It&nbsp;also provided the perfect base 
for algae to anchor&nbsp;itself to the soil. 

00:02:56.667 --> 00:02:58.465
For the first time in Earth's&nbsp;history,&nbsp;

00:02:58.465 --> 00:03:02.697
plants that were trying to grow 
on land&nbsp;didn't have to mine the rock. 

00:03:02.697 --> 00:03:06.400
The&nbsp;nutrients were right there, ready to&nbsp;be used. 

00:03:06.400 --> 00:03:07.554
Because of this, &nbsp;

00:03:07.554 --> 00:03:11.023
the fungi and algae&nbsp;started to conquer the land, 

00:03:11.023 --> 00:03:15.100
building on the soil&nbsp;that 
previous generations made before them. 

00:03:15.100 --> 00:03:21.920
These&nbsp;tiny organisms were about  to evolve 
into&nbsp;every single plant and tree we've ever known. 

