00:04:45.434 --> 00:04:50.247
In order for the first life on&nbsp;land 
to go from algae to the first ever tree, &nbsp;

00:04:50.247 --> 00:04:52.935
some&nbsp;evolutionary breakthroughs had to happen. 

00:04:52.935 --> 00:04:57.600
Although&nbsp;it wasn't optimized for the 
dryness of land and&nbsp;the intense sunlight, 

00:04:57.600 --> 00:05:01.200
very gradually the algae&nbsp;started to evolve. 

00:05:01.200 --> 00:05:03.310
Its cell walls grew thicker 

00:05:03.310 --> 00:05:07.885
and&nbsp;it developed a waxy outer layer 
to stop the water&nbsp;from evaporating. &nbsp;

00:05:07.885 --> 00:05:12.554
This however, made it much harder&nbsp;
for the algae to absorb carbon dioxide

00:05:12.554 --> 00:05:15.235
which it&nbsp;needed for photosynthesis. 

00:05:15.235 --> 00:05:16.320
To get around this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.320 --> 00:05:18.262
it developed tiny little pores 

00:05:18.262 --> 00:05:22.303
that could&nbsp;be opened and closed 
to let the carbon dioxide&nbsp;in

00:05:22.303 --> 00:05:24.948
and to let oxygen out. 

00:05:24.948 --> 00:05:28.694
Instead of relying&nbsp;on a single egg to reproduce, 

00:05:28.694 --> 00:05:31.879
they developed&nbsp;tiny spores at the top of the plant. 

00:05:31.879 --> 00:05:33.755
These would&nbsp;get carried by the wind, 

00:05:33.755 --> 00:05:35.520
land much farther away,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.520 --> 00:05:38.219
and grow into a brand new plant.

00:05:38.219 --> 00:05:41.304
By this point,&nbsp;the algae was no longer algae. 

00:05:41.304 --> 00:05:43.220
It had evolved&nbsp;into an embryophyte &nbsp;

00:05:43.220 --> 00:05:46.431
with tiny leaflike surfaces to&nbsp;absorb light &nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.431 --> 00:05:49.280
and roots that helped anchor it into the soil. 

00:05:49.280 --> 00:05:54.137
Bit by bit, these moss-like&nbsp;plants 
started spreading further inland,&nbsp;

00:05:54.137 --> 00:05:56.619
but&nbsp;without direct access to water, 

00:05:56.619 --> 00:05:59.551
surviving became&nbsp;almost impossible. 

00:05:59.551 --> 00:06:02.067
Once again, they had to evolve.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.067 --> 00:06:03.695
At the bottom of the plant, 

00:06:03.695 --> 00:06:07.405
dead cells started&nbsp;to collect and form into small lumps. 

00:06:07.405 --> 00:06:10.350
Eventually,&nbsp;these grew more and more, &nbsp;

00:06:10.350 --> 00:06:14.793
pushing the leaf higher&nbsp;and 
forming a hollow tube below. 

00:06:14.793 --> 00:06:16.080
Inside the tube,&nbsp;

00:06:16.080 --> 00:06:17.547
water could now be stored 

00:06:17.547 --> 00:06:21.790
and transported&nbsp;all the way from 
the ground up to the plant's&nbsp;leaves. 

00:06:21.790 --> 00:06:25.489
More tubes started branching off from&nbsp;the main stem. 

00:06:25.489 --> 00:06:29.589
And eventually, we arrived at&nbsp;the 
first plant with its own plumbing system,&nbsp;

00:06:29.589 --> 00:06:30.977
 the&nbsp;Cooksonia. 

00:06:30.977 --> 00:06:34.632
No longer did it need to be 
directly on&nbsp;top of a water source.

00:06:34.632 --> 00:06:39.268
Instead, it could&nbsp;spread out 
and conquer the land much more easily,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.268 --> 00:06:43.570
finding pockets of water and 
maximizing&nbsp;its sunlight capture.

00:06:43.570 --> 00:06:46.990
But these tubes also gave&nbsp;the plants some strength, &nbsp;

00:06:46.990 --> 00:06:48.607
and for the first time&nbsp;ever, 

00:06:48.607 --> 00:06:52.206
plants started to fight gravity and&nbsp;grow upwards. &nbsp;

00:06:52.206 --> 00:06:56.404
This started the height war that&nbsp;
would eventually lead to trees. 

00:06:56.404 --> 00:07:00.000
With more&nbsp;&nbsp;and more plants 
fighting for the same resources,&nbsp;

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:04.816
a plant that grew taller than its neighbors would&nbsp;guarantee itself more sunlight

00:07:04.816 --> 00:07:07.641
while also&nbsp;shading its competition. 

00:07:07.641 --> 00:07:08.670
On top of that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.670 --> 00:07:11.361
the reproductive spores from a taller plant 

00:07:11.361 --> 00:07:13.324
would&nbsp;travel farther in the wind, 

00:07:13.324 --> 00:07:18.400
allowing it to quite literally spread 
its seed farther than any other&nbsp;plant. &nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.400 --> 00:07:21.900
But growing tall wasn't easy for these&nbsp;early plants. 

00:07:21.900 --> 00:07:26.060
The vascular tubes that carried the&nbsp;
water were soft and thin. 

00:07:26.060 --> 00:07:27.733
The taller the plant&nbsp;grew, &nbsp;

00:07:27.733 --> 00:07:30.179
the more weight it had to support.

00:07:30.179 --> 00:07:32.179
If they&nbsp;wanted to keep growing taller, 

00:07:32.179 --> 00:07:34.756
they needed a much&nbsp;sturdier material. 

00:07:34.756 --> 00:07:36.618
They needed wood. 

00:07:36.618 --> 00:07:39.342
Luckily, long&nbsp;before algae left the water, 

00:07:39.342 --> 00:07:42.391
it had developed a&nbsp;crucial ingredient called lignin, 

00:07:42.391 --> 00:07:46.279
which protected&nbsp;it from the sun's intense UV radiation. 

00:07:46.279 --> 00:07:48.650
But when&nbsp;plants started conquering land, &nbsp;

00:07:48.650 --> 00:07:55.500
the lignin started bonding together and turning 
the soft&nbsp;plant stems into sturdy, wood-like structures. 

00:07:55.500 --> 00:08:00.000
This&nbsp;breakthrough was about 
to turn plants into giants.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:03.664
But amazingly, one form of life beat them all to&nbsp;it. 

00:08:03.664 --> 00:08:05.590
The prototaxites. 

00:08:05.590 --> 00:08:09.896
While every other plant&nbsp;was 
just starting to reach a few inches&nbsp;tall, 

00:08:09.896 --> 00:08:13.676
prototaxites had already 
established a&nbsp;completely separate 

00:08:13.676 --> 00:08:16.800
but almost identical&nbsp;copy of lignin. 

00:08:16.800 --> 00:08:19.950
This allowed them to grow 
into&nbsp;enormous living beings&nbsp;

00:08:19.950 --> 00:08:23.008
and that had a huge&nbsp;impact on the soil. 

00:08:23.008 --> 00:08:26.648
Their enormous network&nbsp;of inner 
tubes would have loosened up&nbsp;the soil, &nbsp;

00:08:26.648 --> 00:08:28.960
allowing oxygen and water to get in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.960 --> 00:08:30.615
and making it more fertile. 

00:08:30.615 --> 00:08:32.780
Whenever a prototaxite&nbsp;died, 

00:08:32.780 --> 00:08:37.467
it would decompose a huge amount 
of&nbsp;organic matter back into the ground. 

00:08:37.467 --> 00:08:42.532
They weren't&nbsp;alone, but prototaxites 
acted as massive soil producers,

00:08:42.532 --> 00:08:46.921
laying the foundations for the&nbsp;
forests that were about to take over. 

00:08:46.921 --> 00:08:47.840
Eventually,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.840 --> 00:08:49.918
the lignin in plants caught up 

00:08:49.918 --> 00:08:55.154
and these wood-like&nbsp;stems started 
reaching far beyond the heights&nbsp;of prototaxites,

00:08:55.154 --> 00:08:58.100
finally evolving into the Wattieza tree. 

00:08:58.100 --> 00:09:00.835
At this point, trees started taking over

00:09:00.835 --> 00:09:07.734
and lush forests began colonizing 
Earth's&nbsp;surface, reaching far beyond 100 ft tall. 

00:09:07.734 --> 00:09:12.000
Trees&nbsp;had well and truly won the evolutionary race,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.000 --> 00:09:15.300
and prototaxites eventually went extinct.

